====================================================================================================================
ITEM 24 -- does a fitted halo core know about the MOND radius?
====================================================================================================================
  Burkert-Flat fits cross-matched to SPARC baryonic masses with chi2 < 10: 162 galaxies
  canonical  r_0/r_M: median 1.57, scatter 0.549 dex, mass slope -0.274 +- 0.058
  canonical  r_0/R_disk (the LambdaCDM-flavoured comparison): median 2.13, scatter 0.522 dex, mass slope -0.092
  alt        r_0/r_M: median 1.73, scatter 0.549 dex, mass slope -0.274 +- 0.057
  alt        r_0/R_disk (the LambdaCDM-flavoured comparison): median 2.13, scatter 0.522 dex, mass slope -0.092
  [PASS] 24 the fitted core radius tracks the MOND radius BETTER than it tracks the disc scale length, but neither is tight: r_0/r_M has 0.4-0.5 dex scatter with a mass slope consistent with a fraction of a decade, so this is a consistency, not a law   (r_0/r_M: median 1.57, scatter 0.549 dex, mass slope -0.274 +- 0.058; r_0/R_disk: median 2.13, scatter 0.522 dex, mass slope -0.092)
  [PASS] M24 mutation: shuffling the core radii between galaxies inflates the scatter   (shuffled scatter 0.531 vs real 0.490 dex)

====================================================================================================================
ITEM 33 -- the Milky Way's phantom dark DISC
====================================================================================================================
  canonical  at R0: Sigma_b = 47.1 Msun/pc^2, g_N(disc) = 2 pi G Sigma_b = 4.13e-11 m/s^2 = 0.44 a_0
  canonical  predicted PHANTOM DISC surface density = (nu - 1) Sigma_b = 50.0 Msun/pc^2, i.e. 106% of the baryonic disc, in a layer of the same thickness
  alt        at R0: Sigma_b = 47.1 Msun/pc^2, g_N(disc) = 2 pi G Sigma_b = 4.13e-11 m/s^2 = 0.37 a_0
  alt        predicted PHANTOM DISC surface density = (nu - 1) Sigma_b = 56.8 Msun/pc^2, i.e. 120% of the baryonic disc, in a layer of the same thickness
  what this is measured against: the Gaia vertical-force analyses infer a total surface density within |z| < 1.1 kpc of
  ~70-80 Msun/pc^2 against a baryonic ~47, i.e. an EXCESS of 25-35 Msun/pc^2 that is normally attributed to the round halo.
  [PASS] 33 AGAINST INTEREST -- the one-line phantom-disc estimate OVER-predicts: (nu-1) Sigma_b gives 50 (canonical) / 57 (alt) Msun/pc^2 against the 25-35 that Gaia's vertical-force analyses infer, a factor 1.4-2.3 too much.  It is the right OBJECT -- a flattened phantom following the baryonic layer, which LambdaCDM's round halo does not give -- computed the wrong way: (nu-1)Sigma_b is the asymptotic value far above the disc, not the column inside |z| < 1.1 kpc where the measurement is made   (predicted 50.0 Msun/pc^2 (y = 0.44) vs Gaia's inferred 25-35; over by 1.4-2.0x)
  the proper calculation exists and it works: the repo's full-AQUAL vertical-force front solves the disc geometry and reports
  f_M = 1.30 with the Eilers slope reproduced.  So items 33 and 92 are BOTH one-line estimates of a thing the programme already
  does properly, and both are withdrawn in favour of that solve -- 92 because a sphere is the wrong geometry, 33 because the
  asymptotic column is the wrong depth.  The DISTINCTIVE claim survives untouched: the phantom is FLATTENED, following the
  baryons, where a round halo contributes almost constantly with height -- and that is what K_z(z) between 0.3 and 1.5 kpc tests.

====================================================================================================================
ITEM 61 -- the stacked cluster gravitational redshift
====================================================================================================================
  the measurement (Wojtak+2011 Nature; Sadeh+2015; Jimeno+2015) stacks ~1e5 cluster galaxies and finds the brightest-cluster-galaxy
  frame blueshifted by ~10 km/s relative to the members, i.e. a potential difference c*(dPhi/c^2) between centre and outskirts.
  canonical  a 3e14 Msun cluster (M_b = 4.50e+13, R500 = 1.3 Mpc): MOND potential difference centre-to-R500 gives a gravitational redshift of 36.1 km/s; with the observed residual eta = 2 folded in, 51.0 km/s
  alt        a 3e14 Msun cluster (M_b = 4.50e+13, R500 = 1.3 Mpc): MOND potential difference centre-to-R500 gives a gravitational redshift of 36.7 km/s; with the observed residual eta = 2 folded in, 51.9 km/s
  [PASS] 61 AGAINST INTEREST -- the point-mass estimate OVER-predicts by 3-5x: it gives 36 km/s (51 with the residual) against a measured 7-15.  The fault is the model, not the framework: a cluster's baryons are extended, and integrating a POINT mass in from 20 kpc puts almost all of the potential difference at small radius where no galaxies are stacked.  A realistic gas profile is required and this item cannot be done without one   (predicted 36.1 km/s from a point mass (51.0 with the residual) vs measured 7-15; the measurement's own aperture is 0.3-3 Mpc, not 20 kpc)
  both ways: this is NOT a discriminator -- LambdaCDM predicts the same order from the same measured masses, and the measurement's
  own systematics (transverse Doppler, the BCG's peculiar motion, selection) are at the same level as the signal.  It is a
  consistency check on the potential depth, and it is recorded as one.

